      "instructions": "11-14: Choose the correct answer, A, B or C.\n15-20: Label the map below. Choose the correct letter, A-H, for Questions 15-20.",
      "transcript": "NINA: Kia ora, everyone, and welcome to Rimu Downs Sky Night. I’m Nina Harwood, the event leader. We are using a working grass airstrip south of Christchurch, arranged tonight as a one-way observing route. Keep to the marked path and ask a steward before crossing the strip. Although aircraft operations ended this afternoon, uneven ground and darkness still require care.\nNINA: A narrow bank of cloud is forecast around opening time, though the clearer air behind it should arrive soon. If the cloud is still over us at half past seven, we will not cancel or send groups straight to the northern stations. The instruments will stay covered while I spend longer beside the trailer on the safety welcome and a planisphere demonstration. We will release the first rotation only when a useful gap appears.\nNINA: At check-in, each small group receives a numbered wooden disc. Suppose yours is three: carry it along the early route, and when three appears on the telescope board, take it to the circle. At the end of that timed turn, place it in the steward’s return tray so the next group can be called. The untimed binocular stop is the exception; carry the disc through there.\nNINA: Switch ordinary white torches off before leaving the trailer. A bright beam can undo the adjustment your eyes have made and affect people nearby. A deep red filter gives enough light for the arrows while preserving far more of that dark adaptation. Keep phone screens dimmed and inside a red sleeve unless a steward requests one in an emergency.\nNINA: Most instruments stand on grass, which is soft after rain. One visitor tonight uses a mobility aid and cannot safely reach those tripods. Staff will therefore relocate our smallest portable telescope after the gate closes, when vehicle movements have ended and stray light can be screened. A steward will also bring a suitable seat and keep the approach clear. Together, these changes create a stable observing station without adding another lit path.\nNINA: Now follow the map from the south-east arrival lane. The central rectangle is the north-south airstrip. The hedge is west, the open eastern horizon opposite, and the wind sock beyond the northern end. The route runs clockwise and returns down the east edge. Do not cross the central grass.\nNINA: Before joining the dark path, collect any borrowed seat, blanket or star wheel from the table immediately beside the check-in trailer. Damaged equipment is exchanged there; the desk inside handles names only. Then walk towards the south-east corner of the route.\nNINA: At the threshold where the route leaves the arrival side, a steward will inspect torch filters and cover any remaining white LEDs. This checkpoint is before the path bends west, not on the paved return strip. Continue across the bottom of the airstrip until you reach the gap in the western hedge.\nNINA: Just through that south-west gap, low tripod mounts carry the wide-view binoculars. They face the broad southern sky and sit close to the hedge for shelter. Farther north, a recess holds spare folded tripods; that position is marked on the plan but has no activity for visitors tonight, so keep walking past it.\nNINA: The path then reaches a round clearing in the north-west corner. The larger telescopes form an arc there, leaving the centre open for people to change places safely. It may look quicker to approach them from the airstrip, but all groups must enter from the western path and leave across the top.\nNINA: Across the northern end you will see a ground marker for the meteor count. It is near the wind sock but deliberately several paces south of it, so observers can look upward without standing beneath the mast. The spur on the north-east side is reserved for a later horizon talk and is not part of the count.\nNINA: Finally, turn down the eastern return edge. The level viewing patch is at the southern end of that edge, beside the arrival lane and opposite the trailer. Shielding will be ready before the portable instrument arrives. After finishing, continue directly to check-out rather than circling again.\nNINA: Stay with your group, keep voices low near instruments, and report any loose cable or unstable surface to the nearest steward. Warm drinks remain at check-out after the route.",
