      "instructions": "21-25: Who will be responsible for each production step? Choose A, B or C for Questions 21-25. You may use any letter more than once.\n26-30: Choose the correct answer, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "NORA: We have three hours for tomorrow's production practical. Our pair must present seeded loaves, enriched breakfast buns and fruit pastries, but the other pair has booked some of the same equipment. If we follow the recipe folder from top to bottom, we will probably be waiting at the worst possible moments.\nCALEB: Before we build the timetable, let's divide the routine jobs. Last week I tried to prepare two flour batches at once and my totals drifted. You were much steadier at that bench.\nNORA: I'll take the scales, then. I can prepare every set of dry ingredients and seal each one in a labelled container before the first mixer is free.\nCALEB: That leaves the allergen sheet. I can read the codes aloud while you compare them with the containers, although I don't want one person's glance to count as the final check.\nNORA: Agreed. We each need to verify every line and add our initials. If either signature is missing, the assessor will send the batch back before production starts.\nNORA: I can set the proofer when we arrive, but during shaping I'll be facing away from its display. The narrow timing windows worry me more than the initial temperature setting.\nCALEB: I'll stay responsible for that cabinet once the trays enter it. I can note each time and give you a warning before a check is due, even while the pastry cases are resting.\nNORA: The same timing sheet should guide the oven. If two people keep opening its door to add separate trays, the temperature will fall and the sequence will become harder to recover.\nCALEB: Since I'll have those times in front of me, I'll handle the loading as well. You can remain at the bench and finish the next shaping task without crossing the room.\nCALEB: We still need the cooling log. I could note the readings between washing the bowl and cleaning the mixer, but that would split my attention just when the first rack comes out.\nNORA: Leave the temperature record with me. It fits the traceability paperwork I'm already keeping, and you won't have to stop cleaning whenever a product reaches the next check point.\nCALEB: Now for the sequence. I first put the fruit pastries at the top because the cases need chilling. But assembly is quick, and that plan pushes the bulkiest dough much too late.\nNORA: The bun dough is not entirely forgiving either, though it can spend part of its first rise on the warm bench. The seeded batch is different: its schedule allows almost twice as long before it is ready to bake.\nCALEB: Then the seeded batch has to go through the mixer before the others. Its pre-weighed flour saves a few minutes, but that convenience would not matter if the rise were as short as the buns'.\nNORA: Once those first trays are put away to rise, we gain a useful block. The bun glaze would fill only a few minutes of it, whereas the cooked fruit needs time to lose its heat before it can touch the pastry.\nCALEB: So that gap is when we should prepare the filling. Doing it at the very beginning would interrupt the dough setup, and leaving it until the baking is nearly over would make packing impossible.\nNORA: Let's test that timetable against the equipment. The scales were recalibrated yesterday. The proofer looks small, but six trays fit if we stagger the shelves, which covers our peak demand.\nCALEB: The queue forms farther along. Our pair has a single oven, and the three products require separate settings. Even if several trays finish rising together, they cannot all move on at once.\nNORA: We might recover time while portioning the enriched dough. What if I judge the pieces by sight and check only the first one? Repeated weighing will cool the dough a little.\nCALEB: The yeast would survive that, but the portions would vary in weight. The small ones would colour before the large ones had cooked through, and the display would look inconsistent.\nNORA: Fair point. If the clock becomes tight, we can make fewer pastries and still demonstrate every required technique. I would rather not empty the whole container simply to avoid leftover filling.\nCALEB: The marking sheet supports that choice. It rewards consistent colour and crumb across a batch; it does not award extra credit for filling every shelf or using the maximum quantity available.\nNORA: We'll protect that consistency, then. One tray rotation should correct the hot side, and we can reduce the output rather than crowding the oven and getting mixed results.\nCALEB: I'll draft the timeline tonight with equipment settings and target times. Could you add the ingredient quantities and mark where each inspection has to happen?\nNORA: Yes. I'll use separate colours for the three product lines so clashes are visible. Let's also meet ten minutes early in case the other pair has altered the shared-equipment booking.",
