      "title": "Listening — Part 4: academic monologue",
      "instructions": "31-35: Complete the sentences below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.\n36-40: Complete the notes below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "LECTURER: Today we will examine protective packaging in which a fungus helps convert low-value plant matter into a shaped composite. I will begin with the material's structure, move to a simplified laboratory workflow, and finish with one storage limitation. A classroom sequence can clarify relationships without claiming that every factory follows an identical recipe.\nLECTURER: The starting mixture is usually described as a substrate. Rather than using a solid sheet of finished paper, producers may begin with husks, stalk fragments or similar material left after crops are harvested or processed. In materials research, these secondary agricultural streams are commonly grouped under the term residue. They provide both physical bulk and nutrients, although their exact composition depends on the original crop and on what happened during processing.\nLECTURER: The fungus is easy to misunderstand because a mushroom is the part most people recognise. Inside this composite, however, the active structure is made of microscopic filaments. One individual filament is a hypha; in the plural, those fine fungal threads are called hyphae. The distinction matters because hyphae are fungal structures, not plant fibres in the substrate. Together, many hyphae form the mycelium that grows through the material.\nLECTURER: As the filaments extend, they branch around neighbouring particles and cross one another. Separate pieces that could once be poured begin to behave as one body. We can describe the connected arrangement as a binding network. The comparison with glue is only partial: a conventional adhesive is normally introduced as a liquid between surfaces, while here the connections develop as the organism spreads through the available spaces.\nLECTURER: That growth must be brought to an end once the component has filled the intended form. Otherwise its dimensions and surface could continue to change during storage. The research review reports that controlled drying is commonly used to stop fungal growth on the substrate. This also lowers available water, but the effects should not be reversed: the evidence does not establish that only a separate high-temperature stage can end growth.\nLECTURER: A bare composite may cushion an object well while remaining vulnerable to liquid at its outer surface. Designers can respond by applying a thin coating. Such a layer may reduce uptake during brief contact, but it does not make every design waterproof, and its chemistry may alter end-of-life options. The improvement must therefore be evaluated alongside disposal requirements rather than treated as an automatic environmental benefit.\nLECTURER: We can now arrange a hypothetical teaching sequence. It is not attributed to the research review and is not a universal industrial standard. It shows how preparation affects later growth. Changes before incubation can alter how evenly the organism moves through the mixture and how faithfully the material fills a shape. It begins with incoming material and follows changes in particle size, incubation conditions and shape, connecting each preparation choice with a later physical effect.\nLECTURER: First consider substrate selection. The review notes that agricultural wastes differ by type and are selected according to availability and suitability. For this teaching comparison, the incoming plant matter is therefore selected before further preparation; obviously unsuitable foreign pieces are excluded. Washing may remove surface dirt, but it does not decide which plant fraction enters the trial.\nLECTURER: The retained pieces then pass through a cutting mill and are shredded to make their size more consistent. They should not become fine powder, since densely packed dust would leave little air space. Controlled variation is the aim: fragments should mix evenly yet remain open enough for gases to move through the bed.\nLECTURER: Next comes incubation, when the fungus spreads through the prepared substrate. The review identifies pH as one condition that can affect both mycelial growth and colonisation. In a teaching comparison, technicians would therefore record this condition at regular intervals before the developing material moves to the shaping stage; particle size has already been set.\nLECTURER: After a fungal culture has been introduced and has begun to establish, the developing mixture must acquire the geometry of the package. It is placed into moulds that define corners, trays or protective blocks. A flat rack could support a piece later, but it would not impose the required three-dimensional form. At this stage the container is therefore also a shaping tool.\nLECTURER: After the shaped samples have been dried, the final stage is performance testing. One measurement is water absorption: researchers record how much liquid the composite takes up under a stated test condition. The review reports substantial variation between composites and identifies high uptake as an important limitation. This measure concerns direct contact with water; it does not justify a claim that ordinary changes in ambient humidity make a stored component expand or contract.\nLECTURER: The example therefore moves from selecting a usable plant fraction, through particle preparation and controlled incubation, to shaping, colonisation, stabilisation and performance testing. Each transition represents a design choice rather than proof that one recipe suits every fungal strain or crop stream. A route that works for a short, sheltered delivery may be unsuitable for repeated handling or long storage. Comparing several routes is more useful than treating the sequence as a fixed prescription for every crop input and production scale in practice.\nLECTURER: For your seminar, compare the whole material system. Consider the origin of the plant input, the energy used during processing, any added surface treatment, the transport environment and the failure that the package must prevent. A biological ingredient can create useful opportunities, but it does not by itself establish low impact, safe disposal or reliable performance in every application.",
