      "title": "Academic Reading — Passage 2: Designing quiet areas in noisy cities",
      "passageTitle": "Designing quiet areas in noisy cities",
      "sectionStyle": "reading",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A Quiet is not silence\nUrban authorities once treated noise mainly as a number to be reduced. Decibel limits remain useful for controlling harmful exposure, but soundscape researchers ask a different question: how do people experience an acoustic setting? A small garden beside a busy road may not be silent, yet visitors can describe it as restorative when leaves, water and distant human activity form a coherent background. A technically quieter underpass may feel unpleasant if its sounds are unpredictable or signal danger. The distinction shifts attention from sound level alone to meaning, expectation and perceived control.\n\nB Finding candidate areas\nA first survey often combines noise maps with local knowledge. Maps identify places with comparatively low average exposure, while residents point to courtyards, paths and watersides that formal monitoring has overlooked. Researchers then visit at different times. A space that is calm on a Sunday morning may be dominated by deliveries on weekdays. Short measurements are useful, but repeated observation prevents a temporary lull from being mistaken for a stable quiet area. Access, opening hours and nearby land use are recorded alongside sound levels.\n\nC Listening walks\nTeams frequently use guided listening walks. Participants stop at predetermined points, note the sounds they can distinguish and rate qualities such as calmness, vibrancy and safety. The method captures changes along a route rather than reducing a district to one monitor. It also reveals differences among users: birdsong may be welcome to one person but masked by traffic for another. Because a facilitator can influence attention, instructions are kept neutral and the order of stops may be varied between groups.\n\nD What measurements add\nPhysical measurements still matter. Equivalent continuous sound level summarises energy over time, while peak readings capture brief events. Spectral data show whether energy is concentrated in low-frequency traffic rumble or higher-frequency voices and birds. Researchers can also calculate how often a particular source is audible. None of these indicators independently proves restoration. Their value lies in explaining why ratings differ and in allowing planners to test whether an intervention changed the acoustic environment rather than merely its appearance.\n\nE The role of sight and use\nSound is interpreted with other senses. Dense planting may hide a road and reduce visual reminders of traffic even when the measured level changes little. Seating that faces water can focus attention on splashing and conversation. At the same time, a beautiful space that feels unsafe or excludes some users will not function as a refuge. Good assessments therefore record visibility, maintenance, crowding and the activities a place supports. Acoustic quality is one component of a broader experience, not an isolated property of air pressure.\n\nF Testing restoration\nTo investigate restorative effects, studies may ask participants about stress or mood before and after a visit. Some also use simple attention tasks or physiological measures. Results must be interpreted carefully: people choose where to visit, and those already in a better mood may stay longer. Weather, companionship and expectations also influence responses. Randomised laboratory playback can control more variables, but headphones cannot reproduce every visual and social feature of a real park. Field and laboratory methods answer complementary questions.\n\nG Planning a network\nA single flagship park cannot provide everyday relief for an entire city. Planners increasingly consider a network of small, reachable quiet areas linked to walking routes and public transport. Protecting an existing courtyard can be cheaper than constructing a new acoustic barrier. Interventions may include rerouting service vehicles, limiting particular activities at certain times, adding water features or designing planted edges. The aim is not to ban lively sound everywhere; cities need active places as well as calmer alternatives.\n\nH Limits and accountability\nDefinitions should remain transparent. If an authority labels a site quiet solely because its average level falls below a threshold, it may ignore users who find the route inaccessible or threatening. Conversely, satisfaction surveys alone can overlook damaging exposure. A defensible programme publishes both acoustic and experiential criteria, reports when measurements were taken and repeats assessment after changes. There is no universal sound that guarantees wellbeing. The strongest evidence comes from converging measures and from acknowledging whose experience is missing. Long-term stewardship is equally important. A water feature that stops working or planting that blocks sight lines can reverse the qualities recorded in an opening-day survey. Authorities should assign responsibility for maintenance and provide a way for users to report changes. Seasonal audits can then distinguish temporary disturbance from a persistent decline. They also show whether an intervention displaced noise to a neighbouring street. In this sense, a quiet-area label is not a permanent award but a commitment to continued measurement, public access and responsive management.",
